In the below withWatermark function of Dataset.scala:-

def withWatermark(eventTime: String, delayThreshold: String): Dataset[T] = withTypedPlan

{

val parsedDelay = IntervalUtils.fromIntervalString(delayThreshold) require(!IntervalUtils.isNegative(parsedDelay),
s"delay threshold ($delayThreshold) should not be negative.")

EliminateEventTimeWatermark( EventTimeWatermark({color:#172b4d}UnresolvedAttribute(eventTime),{color} parsedDelay, logicalPlan))

}

if we pass number as eventTime parameter. It fails with below ParseException

{color:#de350b}Caused by: org.apache.spark.sql.catalyst.parser.ParseException: {color}
{color:#de350b}Syntax error at or near '1'(line 1, pos 0){color}

{color:#de350b}== SQL =={color}
{color:#de350b}1{color}
{color:#de350b}^^^{color}

{color:#de350b}    at org.apache.spark.sql.catalyst.parser.ParseException.withCommand(ParseDriver.scala:315){color}
{color:#de350b}    at org.apache.spark.sql.catalyst.parser.AbstractSqlParser.parse(ParseDriver.scala:152){color}
{color:#de350b}    at org.apache.spark.sql.catalyst.parser.AbstractSqlParser.parseMultipartIdentifier(ParseDriver.scala:68){color}
{color:#de350b}    at org.apache.spark.sql.catalyst.analysis.UnresolvedAttribute$.apply(unresolved.scala:161){color}
{color:#de350b}    at org.apache.spark.sql.Dataset.withWatermark(Dataset.scala:748){color}

 

In spark 3.1.1 it was able to parse number properly. It was not throwing any error.

The current issue is seen because of the checkin done for the issue https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/SPARK-34636.

The changes made to the {{apply}} method in the {{Unresolved.scala}} file are causing the issue. I have attached the relevant code sections below for Spark 3.1 and Spark 3.2, highlighting the differences.:-

*Spark 3.1*
def apply(name: String): UnresolvedAttribute = {color:#00875a}new UnresolvedAttribute(name.split("
."){color})

*Spark 3.2*
def apply(name: String): UnresolvedAttribute =
    new UnresolvedAttribute(CatalystSqlParser.parseMultipartIdentifier(name))

Please visit this link to see the code changes:- [https://github.com/apache/spark/commit/94fd6cb0ce56684de47eb5cca925055859c968ef]

In above code for spark 3.1, we can see that in object instantiation of the apply Method only name.split is being done.
But with the above mentioned changed in spark 3.2 code and in later versions of spark  CatalystSqlParser.parseMultipartIdentifier(name) is being called.
In turn this parseMultipartIdentifier method will call singleMultipartIdentifier method of SQLParser. we can see in the below code snippet.

override def parseMultipartIdentifier(sqlText: String): Seq[String] = {
parse(sqlText) { parser =>
astBuilder.visitSingleMultipartIdentifier({color:#4c9aff}parser.singleMultipartIdentifier(){color})
}
}

Now, once {color:#4c9aff}parser.singleMultipartIdentifier(){color} method is called. It will try to validate sqlText using the singleMultipartIdentifier rule. This sqlText parameter will contain the same value which we passed to eventTime  parameter of withWaterMark function. 
Now when this sqlText is a number(For Example "1"), than it will throw {color:#de350b}ParseException{color} which i mentioned in the beginning, as singleMultipartIdentifier rule can't parse Integer Value (numbers).

 

 

Possible Solution that i can suggest: 
Introduce a new method in object of UnresolvedAttribute as shown below

object UnresolvedAttribute {

{color:#172b4d}def numberString(name:String){color}

{color:#172b4d}: UnresolvedAttribute = new UnresolvedAttribute(name.split("{color}
."))

}

and in withWatermark function of Dataset.scala do a function call to UnresolvedAttribute.numberString(eventTime) inplace of calling a constructor UnresolvedAttribute(eventTime) as shown below

def withWatermark(eventTime: String, delayThreshold: String): Dataset[T] = withTypedPlan

{

val parsedDelay = IntervalUtils.fromIntervalString(delayThreshold) require(!IntervalUtils.isNegative(parsedDelay), 
s"delay threshold ($delayThreshold) should not be negative.")

EliminateEventTimeWatermark( EventTimeWatermark({color:#4c9aff}UnresolvedAttribute.numberString(eventTime),{color} parsedDelay, logicalPlan))

}

Code changes might be needed in the following locations::-
 # //branch/sql/catalyst/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/catalyst/analysis/unresolved.scala
 # //branch/sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/Dataset.scala

I haven't looked into how _general_ this problem is, but here's a very specific scenario which I ran into last night.

 

When the `{{{}SparkSession{}}}` is created _without_ specifying the config `{{{}spark.sql.warehouse.sql{}}}`, the default value is _cwd/spark-warehouse_ and this path seems URL-encoded when printed via `spark.conf.get('spark.sql.warehouse.dir')`.

So, for instance, if any spaces were present in the path, they will be replaced by "%20".

If this is the case, then the path should be decoded whenever necessary, but it turns out this encoded path is taken literally and consequently spark writes tables to a different location than intended.

 

here's a minimal snippet to reproduce:

```py

{{from pyspark.sql import SparkSession}}

{{spark = SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()}}
 
{{spark.conf.get('spark.sql.warehouse.dir') # 'file:/Users/user/cwd%20with%20space/spark-warehouse'}}
 
{{df = ...}}
{{df.write.saveAsTable('df') # table will be saved at /Users/user/cwd%20with%20space/spark-warehouse}}
```

 

Interestingly, this doesn't happen if the path is manually specified when creating the session. Even if the path is literally the same as what spark would've taken by-default.

 

```py

{{from pyspark.sql import SparkSession}}

{{{}spark = SparkSession.builder.config('{}}}spark.sql.warehouse.dir', 'spark-warehouse/').getOrCreate()

 
{{spark.conf.get('spark.sql.warehouse.dir') # 'file:/Users/user/cwd with space/spark-warehouse'}}
```

 

The above works fine.

 

PS. plz forgive me if this is supposed to happen by design

Since IDENTIFIER clause was added a lot of gaps remain. One of them is SET CATALOG syntax added in https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/SPARK-36841

Now {{SET CATALOG IDENTIFIER('test')}} will fall with
{code:java}
[INVALID_SET_SYNTAX] Expected format is 'SET', 'SET key', or 'SET key=value'...{code}
Parser can't match {{SET CATALOG}} with an identifier but can match next "setConfiguration" grammar, what makes the error even more confusing.

It would be nice to support identifier in this context as well.

Spark on K8S supports mounting volumes to driver and executor pods. It supports the {{subPath}} but not the {{subPathExpr}} K8S option, which allows using environment variables for the {{subPath}}. Further, hostPath volumes are always mounted as default type {{""}}. Providing a different hostPath volume type allows for more flexibility.

Add the following configuration options:
- {{spark.kubernetes.executor.volumes.[VolumeType].[VolumeName].mount.subPathExpr}}
- {{spark.kubernetes.executor.volumes.hostPath.[VolumeName].options.type}}

