I don't think there are regression risk to close correctly the URLClassLoader at the end, because everything should be already stopped, and no new class would be defined after.

if a schema ddl_string is passed in via createDataFrame
{code:python}
data = [{"id": 1, "value": 0}]
spark.createDataFrame(data, schema="id long, value double")
{code}
then it does not pass that through to spark connect, but instead *always* passes in the inferred schema as a json string.

a few notable issues: 
we can see the branch where the schema is passed in [here|https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/6add9c89855f9311d5e185774ddddcbf4323beee/python/pyspark/sql/connect/session.py#L467]

which eagerly calls the connect analyze method, but then the evaluated schema is only used if it's [pandas|https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/6add9c89855f9311d5e185774ddddcbf4323beee/python/pyspark/sql/connect/session.py#L523] or if its a [pyarrow table |https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/6add9c89855f9311d5e185774ddddcbf4323beee/python/pyspark/sql/connect/session.py#L604]

but never when you pass in [python literals|https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/6add9c89855f9311d5e185774ddddcbf4323beee/python/pyspark/sql/connect/session.py#L659]

for python literals it seems to always match on [this branch|https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/6add9c89855f9311d5e185774ddddcbf4323beee/python/pyspark/sql/connect/session.py#L709] which passes in the inferred schema.

it appears to do this because of the schema check
{code:python}
        # schema from spark.createDataFrame(data, schema="id long, value double") is a string
        if isinstance(schema, str):
            schema = self.client._analyze(  # type: ignore[assignment]
                method="ddl_parse", ddl_string=schema
            ).parsed

        # the schema from the client is StructType, so this always matches
        if isinstance(schema, (AtomicType, StructType)):
            _schema = schema
            if isinstance(schema, StructType):
                _num_cols = len(schema.fields)
            else:
                _num_cols = 1
        
        # but then down on line 708, we check _schema, which will always be Some when a string is passed in.
        if _schema is not None:
            local_relation = LocalRelation(_table, schema=_schema.json())
        else:
            local_relation = LocalRelation(_table)
{code}
so for summary
 - the connect.analyze method should not be eagerly called if the schema string is passed in, and its using python literals. It should only be evaluated when needed for pandas and pyarrow.
 - the schema string should be passed in to `LocalRelation` instead of the inferred json schema when provided.

